<h2>Topic 2: Types of Pension Schemes - DB vs DC, NSSF, Occupational</h2><p><b>Estimated time:</b> ~35 minutes | <b>Objectives:</b> Distinguish DB vs DC, explain NSSF, explain occupational and personal schemes.</p>
<p>The big divide is who bears the risk.</p><div class='keybox'><p><b>DB - Defined Benefit:</b> Pension = Formula, e.g. 1/60 x Final Salary x Years. Employer bears investment + longevity risk. Example: Final salary 1200000, years 30, accrual 1/60. Pension = 600000 per year.</p><p><b>DC - Defined Contribution:</b> Contributions fixed, e.g. 10% employee + 10% employer. Pension = Fund / Annuity Factor. Member bears risk.</p><p><b>NSSF:</b> Mandatory for all workers in Kenya. Employer + employee contribute. Pays retirement, invalidity, survivors benefits.</p><p><b>Occupational:</b> Employer scheme, e.g. for teachers, bank staff. May be DB or DC.</p></div>
